::ENG3_U02_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A school removes an old mural. Alia helped paint it and sees the removal as loss. Mateo is new and sees the blank wall as a chance to belong. Which detail best establishes Alia's perspective? {
=Alia recognizes her painted handprint beneath the first coat of white paint.#Correct. The detail shows personal connection and loss.
~Mateo opens a box of new brushes.#This establishes Mateo's perspective.
~The hallway has lockers.#This is setting, not perspective.
~The wall is rectangular.#This detail is too general.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A school removes an old mural. Alia helped paint it and sees the removal as loss. Mateo is new and sees the blank wall as a chance to belong. Which detail best establishes Mateo's perspective? {
~Alia remembers painting after school.#This establishes Alia's perspective.
=Mateo imagines adding a corner where new students can sign their names.#Correct. This shows Mateo's hope for belonging.
~The paint dries by noon.#This is neutral.
~The hallway is quiet.#This could create mood but does not clearly show Mateo's perspective.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A school removes an old mural. Alia helped paint it and sees the removal as loss. Mateo is new and sees the blank wall as a chance to belong. Which sentence best uses juxtaposition? {
~The wall was in the hallway near the office.#This gives location, not contrast.
~The mural had colors, and the wall had paint.#This is too vague.
=Alia saw an erased memory, while Mateo saw an empty space waiting for a first mark.#Correct. This places contrasting perspectives side by side.
~Mateo and Alia were students at school.#This states a basic fact.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A school removes an old mural. Alia helped paint it and sees the removal as loss. Mateo is new and sees the blank wall as a chance to belong. Which structure would best support multiple perspectives? {
~Only Alia narrates every thought and event.#This would not show both perspectives fully.
~The story lists paint colors with no characters.#This does not establish perspectives.
~The story gives no transition between viewpoints.#This could confuse readers.
=The story uses one section labeled Alia and one section labeled Mateo.#Correct. Clear sections help readers follow viewpoint shifts.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A school removes an old mural. Alia helped paint it and sees the removal as loss. Mateo is new and sees the blank wall as a chance to belong. Which sentence best revises for clarity? {
~Alia and Mateo felt stuff about the wall.#This is vague.
=Alia grieved the mural because it held her history, but Mateo welcomed the blank wall because it offered him a place to begin.#Correct. This clearly explains both perspectives.
~The wall was there and then it was different.#This is unclear.
~Everyone had emotions because the paint changed.#This is too general.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A town replaces an old footbridge. Mina values the bridge because it holds family memories. Jon supports replacing it because it is unsafe. Which shared event should the narrative focus on? {
~Mina's entire childhood.#This is too broad for one shared event.
~Jon's unrelated job interview.#This does not connect to both perspectives.
=The replacement of the old footbridge.#Correct. This event matters to both characters.
~A weather report from another town.#This does not fit the situation.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A town replaces an old footbridge. Mina values the bridge because it holds family memories. Jon supports replacing it because it is unsafe. Which detail best shows Mina's perspective? {
~Jon checks the bridge bolts.#This shows Jon's safety concern.
=Mina touches the initials her grandfather carved into the rail.#Correct. This detail shows memory and attachment.
~The bridge crosses water.#This is setting, not perspective.
~Workers arrive with tools.#This advances the event but not Mina's perspective.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A town replaces an old footbridge. Mina values the bridge because it holds family memories. Jon supports replacing it because it is unsafe. Which detail best shows Jon's perspective? {
~Mina remembers walking with her grandfather.#This shows Mina's perspective.
~The river moves under the bridge.#This is neutral setting.
=Jon counts three cracked boards near the school-bus stop.#Correct. This detail shows his safety concern.
~Mina keeps a photograph.#This shows Mina's memory.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A town replaces an old footbridge. Mina values the bridge because it holds family memories. Jon supports replacing it because it is unsafe. Which sentence uses figurative language to reveal Mina's view? {
~The bridge had boards and rails.#This is literal and general.
~Jon inspected the boards carefully.#This reveals Jon, not Mina.
~The town scheduled construction on Monday.#This is factual.
=To Mina, the bridge was a wooden scrapbook stretched across the river.#Correct. The metaphor shows memory and attachment.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A town replaces an old footbridge. Mina values the bridge because it holds family memories. Jon supports replacing it because it is unsafe. Which comparison best supports a complex narrative? {
~Mina and Jon both look at a bridge.#This is too basic.
=Mina sees the bridge as memory, while Jon sees it as risk.#Correct. This contrast reveals different perspectives.
~The bridge is old, and the river is wet.#This does not show perspective.
~Construction workers use tools.#This is not a perspective comparison.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A family sells an old house. Lena sees the sale as losing home. Her brother Sam sees it as relief from constant repairs. Which detail best reveals Lena's perspective? {
=Lena presses a loose stair board because it still squeaks like it did when she was six.#Correct. This detail shows memory and attachment.
~Sam stacks repair bills on the counter.#This shows Sam's perspective.
~The house has windows.#This is too general.
~The realtor arrives at noon.#This advances the event but does not show Lena's view.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A family sells an old house. Lena sees the sale as losing home. Her brother Sam sees it as relief from constant repairs. Which detail best reveals Sam's perspective? {
~Lena studies old pencil marks on the wall.#This shows Lena's attachment.
~The house is located near trees.#This is setting.
=Sam circles the cost of the roof repair and exhales for the first time that morning.#Correct. This shows relief connected to repair burdens.
~A door is painted green.#This does not clearly show perspective.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A family sells an old house. Lena sees the sale as losing home. Her brother Sam sees it as relief from constant repairs. Which transition best signals a shift in perspective? {
~The house was old.#This gives description, not a viewpoint shift.
=Across the room, Sam saw the same walls differently.#Correct. This signals a shift from one perspective to another.
~It was a house.#This is too vague.
~There were many repairs.#This provides information but not a transition.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A family sells an old house. Lena sees the sale as losing home. Her brother Sam sees it as relief from constant repairs. Which draft sentence is weakest for perspective? {
~Lena folded the old porch key into her palm.#This can show attachment.
~Sam studied the repair bills.#This can show financial concern.
=The house was a building with rooms.#Correct. This is too general and does not reveal perspective.
~Lena looked at the bedroom doorway where her height marks remained.#This shows memory.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A family sells an old house. Lena sees the sale as losing home. Her brother Sam sees it as relief from constant repairs. Which revision best improves structure? {
~Lena remembered things Sam had bills and the house sold.#This is unclear and compressed.
~The old house had a lot of things happen there.#This is vague.
~Sam and Lena were different about it.#This tells difference without showing it.
=First show Lena's memory of the house, then shift to Sam's concern about repairs.#Correct. This structure clearly organizes both perspectives.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A community garden is replaced by a parking lot. Rae sees the garden as a place of belonging. Devon sees the parking lot as needed access for older residents. Which detail best reveals Rae's perspective? {
~Devon counts the number of accessible parking spaces.#This shows Devon's perspective.
=Rae finds the tomato marker with her neighbor's handwriting still on it.#Correct. This detail shows community memory and belonging.
~The parking lot has painted lines.#This is neutral unless tied to a view.
~A sign is posted near the sidewalk.#This is setting information.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A community garden is replaced by a parking lot. Rae sees the garden as a place of belonging. Devon sees the parking lot as needed access for older residents. Which detail best reveals Devon's perspective? {
~Rae saves a packet of seeds.#This shows Rae's attachment.
~Rae remembers watering plants with neighbors.#This shows Rae's perspective.
~The garden soil is dark.#This is descriptive but not clearly Devon's view.
=Devon watches his grandmother struggle to walk from the far bus stop.#Correct. This detail explains why access matters to Devon.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A community garden is replaced by a parking lot. Rae sees the garden as a place of belonging. Devon sees the parking lot as needed access for older residents. Which sentence best shows balanced multiple perspectives? {
~Rae was right and Devon was wrong.#This removes complexity.
~Devon wanted a parking lot because gardens are pointless.#This oversimplifies Devon.
=Rae grieved the garden's shared history, while Devon hoped the new lot would help neighbors who could not walk far.#Correct. This fairly shows both perspectives.
~The garden was replaced and people talked.#This is vague.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A community garden is replaced by a parking lot. Rae sees the garden as a place of belonging. Devon sees the parking lot as needed access for older residents. Which choice would confuse the reader most? {
~Using a section break before Devon's perspective.#This can help clarity.
=Switching from Rae's thoughts to Devon's thoughts with no signal.#Correct. Unmarked shifts can confuse readers.
~Giving each character a specific detail to notice.#This supports clarity.
~Keeping the shared event the same.#This helps the narrative stay focused.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: A community garden is replaced by a parking lot. Rae sees the garden as a place of belonging. Devon sees the parking lot as needed access for older residents. Which sentence best uses contrast? {
~The garden was green and the parking lot was paved.#This is visual contrast but does not reveal both perspectives deeply.
=Rae saw an uprooted neighborhood; Devon saw a shorter walk for people who had been left at the curb.#Correct. This contrasts values and perspectives.
~There were plants and cars.#This is too simple.
~The project happened in spring.#This gives time, not perspective.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: Two cousins watch the family bakery close. Mara wants to preserve tradition. Eli wants to use the building for a tutoring center. Which detail best supports Mara's perspective? {
=Mara reads the flour-dusted recipe card taped inside the cabinet.#Correct. The recipe card shows tradition and family history.
~Eli measures the back room for desks.#This supports Eli's perspective.
~The building has a front door.#This is too general.
~The sidewalk is uneven.#This does not show Mara's perspective.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: Two cousins watch the family bakery close. Mara wants to preserve tradition. Eli wants to use the building for a tutoring center. Which detail best supports Eli's perspective? {
~Mara saves an old apron.#This supports Mara's perspective.
~Mara remembers morning bread.#This shows tradition.
=Eli imagines a table where students can finish homework after school.#Correct. This shows his future-focused purpose.
~The bakery sign is red.#This is not enough to reveal Eli's view.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: Two cousins watch the family bakery close. Mara wants to preserve tradition. Eli wants to use the building for a tutoring center. Which sentence most clearly establishes multiple perspectives? {
~The bakery was old and important.#This is not specific enough.
~Mara and Eli were cousins.#This states relationship, not perspective.
~The building could be used somehow.#This is vague.
=Mara saw the bakery as inheritance; Eli saw the same rooms as a chance to serve students.#Correct. This sentence clearly contrasts perspectives.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: Two cousins watch the family bakery close. Mara wants to preserve tradition. Eli wants to use the building for a tutoring center. Which convention revision is best? Draft: Mara held the recipe card Eli opened his notebook. {
~Mara held, the recipe card Eli opened, his notebook.#The commas interrupt the sentence incorrectly.
~Mara held the recipe card Eli opened his notebook.#This remains a run-on.
=Mara held the recipe card, and Eli opened his notebook.#Correct. The comma and conjunction correctly join the two independent clauses.
~Mara held the recipe card, Eli opened his notebook.#This is a comma splice.
}

::ENG3_U02_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG3.W.01] Narrative situation: Two cousins watch the family bakery close. Mara wants to preserve tradition. Eli wants to use the building for a tutoring center. Which final sentence best preserves complexity? {
~Eli was right, so Mara stopped caring.#This oversimplifies Mara.
~Mara was right, so Eli gave up.#This oversimplifies Eli.
=Mara locked the recipe card in the drawer while Eli taped a tutoring schedule beside it.#Correct. This ending holds both memory and new purpose together.
~The bakery closed and nothing mattered.#This drops both perspectives.
}
